<chapter xml:id="ch-validity-interpretation">
  <title>Validity and Interpretation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      The preceding chapters built a framework for representing,
      reconstructing, summarizing, visualizing, and modeling drag-and-drop
      interaction data. The modeling chapter ended with a restraint that belongs
      at the center of the whole framework: a stable or predictive pattern is
      not automatically a claim about student thinking. This final chapter makes
      that boundary explicit. The framework measures observable structure in
      responses and <xref ref="gt-trajectory" text="custom">trajectories</xref>.
      Claims about reasoning, misconception, strategy, attention, or learning
      require an additional validity argument.
    </p>

    <p>
      The central distinction is between what the data record and what an
      analyst infers from those records. A logged
      <xref ref="gt-move" text="custom">move</xref> records that a
      <xref ref="gt-card" text="custom">card</xref> changed
      <xref ref="gt-zone" text="custom">zones</xref>. A
      <xref ref="gt-check" text="custom">check</xref> records that a particular
      <xref ref="gt-configuration" text="custom">configuration</xref> was
      submitted to the grader. A <xref ref="gt-reset" text="custom">reset</xref>
      records that the <xref ref="gt-board" text="custom">board</xref> returned
      to the <xref ref="gt-initial-state" text="custom">initial state</xref>.
      These are direct observations. Statements such as <q>the student guessed</q>,
      <q>the student misunderstood the category</q>, or <q>feedback caused a
      repair</q> are interpretations layered on top of those observations.
    </p>

    <p>
      The purpose of this chapter is not to discourage interpretation.
      Interpretation is the reason to build the framework. The purpose is to make
      interpretation disciplined: structural pattern first, possible explanation
      second, validating evidence third. The chapter first collects what the
      framework measures directly and what it does not; then gives a claim ladder
      for moving from structure to interpretation; then treats the two design
      conditions that most strongly shape interpretation, the
      <xref ref="gt-feedback-policy" text="custom">feedback policy</xref> and the
      <xref ref="gt-item-design" text="custom">item design</xref>; then catalogs
      validation strategies; and finally looks back across the framework.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-what-framework-measures-directly">
    <title>What the Framework Measures Directly</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The framework directly measures properties of item designs, board states,
        event logs, reconstructed trajectories, and derived structural features.
        These quantities are observable or mechanically derived from observable
        records. Each was defined earlier; this section collects them by level.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-direct-item-measures">
      <title>Item Design</title>

      <p>
        At the design level, the framework records the task structure and the
        covariates of <xref ref="tab-item-design-covariates"/>: the number of
        cards <m>P</m> and <xref ref="gt-slot" text="custom">slots</xref>
        <m>R</m>, the zone set <m>\mathcal{Z}</m> with its distinguished
        <xref ref="gt-tray" text="custom">tray</xref>, the key configuration
        <m>s^\star</m>, the decoy-card and decoy-slot sets, source-order labels,
        the <xref ref="gt-item-map" text="custom">item map</xref>, and the
        feedback policy <m>\phi</m> when known.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are properties of the item, not the student. They describe the
        response space and the conditions under which every trajectory in the
        data was produced. For that reason, they must accompany any interpretation
        of those trajectories.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-direct-state-measures">
      <title>States and Checked Responses</title>

      <p>
        At the state level, the framework represents where the cards are by a
        configuration and its <xref ref="gt-membership-matrix" text="custom">
        membership matrix</xref>:
        <md>
          s:\mathcal{P}\to\mathcal{Z},
          \qquad
          X=X(s).
        </md>
        From a <xref ref="gt-state" text="custom">state</xref>, it computes
        structural comparisons with the <xref ref="gt-key" text="custom">key</xref>:
        the agreement score <m>\sigma(X)</m>, the key distance
        <m>d^\star(X)</m>, the <xref ref="gt-occupancy" text="custom">occupancy</xref>
        vector <m>\mathbf{o}(X)=X\mathbf{1}_P</m>, the error profile
        <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}(X)</m>, and the label-free and grouping
        summaries of <xref ref="ch-group-features"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        These quantities describe the relationship between a submitted state and
        the key. They do not, by themselves, explain why that state occurred.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-direct-trajectory-measures">
      <title>Events and Trajectories</title>

      <p>
        At the trajectory level, the framework records or reconstructs the
        marked, timestamped trajectory of
        <xref ref="an-event-trajectory" text="custom">the trajectory model</xref>:
        <md>
          \Gamma
          =
          \bigl(
          (a_0,t_0,X_0),
          (a_1,t_1,X_1),
          \dots,
          (a_n,t_n,X_n)
          \bigr).
        </md>
        From <m>\Gamma</m>, it computes the process features of
        <xref ref="ch-baseline-features"/>: move, check, reset, and
        <xref ref="gt-takeback" text="custom">takeback</xref> counts; distance
        curves; fixing, lateral, and breaking moves; excess moves; repeated
        states; check cadence; post-check gains; action gaps; and active time.
      </p>

      <p>
        These are process measures. They summarize what happened in the
        interface. In the register fixed when the distance curve was introduced,
        they are movement descriptions, not direct evidence of a particular
        reasoning process (<xref ref="subsec-distance-curve" text="title"/>).
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-direct-cohort-measures">
      <title>Cohort-Level Summaries</title>

      <p>
        At the cohort level, the framework aggregates states and features. The
        placement heatmap
        (<xref ref="an-baseline-placement-heatmap" text="custom">the cohort
        feature definition</xref>)
        <md>
          \bar{X}=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{\nu=1}^{N}X^{(\nu)}
        </md>
        records empirical placement proportions; the mean zone confusion matrix
        <m>\bar{M}</m> records average source-target placement patterns; and
        the item-level diagnostic summaries of
        <xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/> record rates, distributions,
        and quality-control counts.
      </p>

      <p>
        These summaries describe observed behavior in a cohort. They should not
        be treated as stable item properties until they have been checked across
        cohorts, semesters, item versions, or feedback policies. That stability
        problem is one of the reasons the modeling and validation layers are
        needed.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-what-framework-does-not-measure-directly">
    <title>What the Framework Does Not Measure Directly</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The framework does not observe cognition. It observes interaction traces.
        This limitation should be stated whenever features are used to support
        claims about student reasoning. The four subsections below name common
        overextensions.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-not-direct-understanding">
      <title>Understanding and Misconceptions</title>

      <p>
        A wrong placement does not directly measure a misconception. A
        <xref ref="gt-decoy-card-leak" text="custom">decoy-card leak</xref>
        records that a card meant to remain in the tray was placed into a slot;
        a <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot-leak" text="custom">decoy-slot leak</xref>
        records that a slot meant to remain empty received a card. These patterns
        may reflect a content misconception, an interface assumption such as
        <q>every card must be placed</q>, guessing, or strategic probing of the
        grader. The log alone does not decide among those explanations.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conversely, a correct placement does not prove understanding. A student
        may place a card correctly through reasoning, memory, elimination,
        <xref ref="gt-feedback" text="custom">feedback</xref>, guessing, or
        copying. The framework can record that a state is correct and describe
        the path that produced it. Understanding requires additional evidence.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-not-direct-strategy">
      <title>Strategy and Intent</title>

      <p>
        Process features can suggest strategies, but they do not identify intent.
        Frequent checking may indicate guessing, careful incremental use of
        feedback, low confidence, or an efficient strategy under the item's check
        policy. A long action gap may reflect thinking, distraction,
        interruption, or a page left open, which is why the processing pipeline
        flags long idle gaps rather than interpreting them. A reset may be a
        deliberate fresh start, confusion, or an accidental click.
      </p>

      <p>
        Labels such as <q>guessing</q>, <q>systematic reasoning</q>,
        <q>trial and error</q>, and <q>feedback seeking</q> are interpretive
        codes, not measurements, until additional evidence supports them.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-not-direct-learning">
      <title>Learning</title>

      <p>
        Improvement within a trajectory is not automatically learning. A student
        may move closer to the key after feedback because the feedback revealed
        the error, because the student reconsidered the content, because the
        student guessed again, or because the policy highlighted the relevant
        card.
      </p>

      <p>
        Learning claims require evidence beyond within-item progress: transfer to
        later items, pre/post comparisons, retention, explanations, interviews,
        or consistency across related tasks. The trajectory can document
        improvement on the item. It does not, by itself, establish learning beyond
        the item.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-not-direct-causality">
      <title>Causality</title>

      <p>
        The framework does not by itself establish causal effects. An association
        between checking and eventual success does not show that checking caused
        success: students who check more may differ from students who check less,
        and items that invite checking may differ in difficulty, feedback, or
        design. The modeling chapter made the same point about coefficients
        (<xref ref="subsec-random-effects-interpretation" text="title"/>).
      </p>

      <p>
        Causal claims require a design or analysis capable of supporting them:
        randomized feedback conditions, designed contrasts, longitudinal evidence,
        or explicit causal assumptions. Without such support, model coefficients
        are associations.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-structural-patterns-to-interpretations">
    <title>From Structural Patterns to Interpretations</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The framework is most useful when it supports a disciplined movement from
        observed structure to possible interpretation. That movement should be
        explicit in the language of analysis and reporting.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-claim-ladder">
      <title>A Claim Ladder</title>

      <p>
        A useful validity discipline is to organize claims by level. For the
        running <xref ref="gt-attempt" text="custom">attempt</xref> of
        <xref ref="tab-raw-data-trace"/>, the levels look like this:
      </p>

      <p>
        <dl width="medium">
          <li>
            <title>Level 1: observation</title>
            <p>
              What did the log record? The student moved card <m>c_4</m>, the
              circle, from the tray to zone <m>z_3</m>, the Pentagon slot.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 2: structural feature</title>
            <p>
              What feature does the framework compute? The move created both a
              decoy-card leak and a decoy-slot leak, so the next checked state
              has error profile
              <m>\boldsymbol{\varepsilon}=(0,0,1,1)</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 3: response pattern</title>
            <p>
              What pattern appears across states, attempts, or students? Many
              students placed the <xref ref="gt-decoy-card" text="custom">decoy
              card</xref> into the same <xref ref="gt-decoy-slot" text="custom">decoy
              slot</xref>, the pattern made visible by the heatmap and decoy-pull
              diagnostics (<xref ref="subsec-decoy-pull" text="title"/>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 4: interpretation</title>
            <p>
              What might the pattern mean? The item may invite an <q>every card
              must be placed</q> or <q>every slot must be filled</q> assumption.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <title>Level 5: validated claim</title>
            <p>
              What additional evidence supports that interpretation? Possible
              evidence includes student explanations, comparison items, stable
              cross-semester patterns, or an experimental manipulation of the
              decoy.
            </p>
          </li>
        </dl>
      </p>

      <p>
        The framework directly supports the first three levels. The fourth is an
        interpretation. The fifth requires a validity argument, and the strategies
        of <xref ref="sec-validation-strategies"/> are ways to assemble one.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-interpretive-language">
      <title>Interpretive Language</title>

      <p>
        Reporting language should match the strength of the evidence. Structural
        claims can be stated directly:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Thirty percent of final checks placed the decoy card into an
              authored slot</q> (the decoy-card leak rate of the illustrative
              tallies in <xref ref="tab-cheat-tallies"/>).
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>The most common first-check error was a merge of the two home
              groups.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>Students who checked after each move had lower post-check gains
              on this item.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Interpretive claims should be hedged unless validated:
        <ul>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>This pattern is consistent with an assumption that every card
              should be placed.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>The trajectory suggests feedback-guided revision.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              <q>The repeated use of the decoy slot may indicate that the category
              label is attracting responses.</q>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </p>

      <p>
        Stronger language, such as <q>students misunderstood</q> or
        <q>feedback caused learning</q>, should be reserved for cases where the
        evidence goes beyond the interaction trace.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-triangulation">
      <title>Triangulation</title>

      <p>
        Structural patterns become more interpretable when triangulated with other
        evidence: student explanations, written work, think-aloud protocols,
        interviews, related items measuring the same idea, pre/post or
        delayed-transfer items, instructor knowledge of the course context, or
        experimental variation in feedback, wording, or layout.
      </p>

      <p>
        Triangulation does not require every study to collect every kind of
        evidence. It requires the strength of the claim to match the strength of
        the evidence available.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-feedback-policy-construct-validity">
    <title>Feedback Policy and Construct Validity</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        The feedback policy <m>\phi</m> was introduced as part of the item design
        (<xref ref="sec-feedback-policy"/>). It is a rule applied at check events,
        with possible inputs such as the checked configuration and check history,
        and possible outputs ranging from an all-or-nothing verdict to counts,
        card-level highlighting, or withheld feedback. It reappears here because
        it is part of the construct being measured. The same response pattern can
        mean different things under different policies. For example, a change from
        always-on card-level feedback to a gated, layered policy changes the task
        students experience, even if the source question and key are otherwise the
        same (<xref ref="subsec-platform-feedback-policies" text="title"/>).
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feedback-changes-task">
      <title>Feedback Changes the Task</title>

      <p>
        A drag-and-drop item with immediate, card-level feedback is not the same
        task as the same board with a single all-or-nothing verdict, even when
        <m>P</m>, <m>R</m>, and the key are identical. In the first case, students
        can revise under guidance during the attempt; in the second, each checked
        state is closer to an unaided response at that moment. The feedback
        covariates of <xref ref="tab-feedback-covariates"/> record which task the
        student actually received: granularity, content, timing, check limits,
        availability, consequence, and highlighting.
      </p>

      <p>
        Features such as <c>num_checks</c>, <c>post_check_gain</c>, and
        <c>responsiveness</c> must therefore be interpreted relative to
        <m>\phi</m>. A high check count may be wasteful in one feedback
        environment and strategically reasonable in another. This is the design
        chapter's feedback-dependence point applied at the validity level
        (<xref ref="subsec-feedback-dependent-features" text="title"/>).
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feedback-sensitive-features-validity">
      <title>Feedback-Sensitive Features</title>

      <p>
        Some features exist only under some policies. Responsiveness
        <m>\rho_i</m> compares the highlighted set <m>\mathcal{H}_i</m> to the
        cards moved before the next check
        (<xref ref="an-baseline-responsiveness" text="custom">the baseline
        definition</xref>). If the policy returns only Correct or Incorrect, then
        <m>\mathcal{H}_i</m> is not observed and the feature is undefined, not
        zero. Likewise, the grader counts <m>w(X)</m> and <m>u(X)</m> are exactly
        what a count-level policy might disclose; their interpretation as
        <em>seen information</em> depends on whether the policy disclosed them.
      </p>

      <p>
        Feedback-sensitive features should be reported with the policy that makes
        them meaningful. If the policy differs across items or semesters, an
        analysis should model that difference or decline to compare the feature
        across policies.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feedback-and-outcome-interpretation">
      <title>Outcome Interpretation Under Feedback</title>

      <p>
        Final correctness after multiple checks is an outcome of an interactive
        environment. It mixes initial knowledge with feedback, persistence,
        revision, and the item's check policy. First-check correctness is closer
        to an initial response. The two are different constructs, and the
        diagnostic chapter's insistence on naming the analysis point
        (<xref ref="subsec-heatmap-definition" text="title"/>) is the display
        version of the same validity requirement.
      </p>

      <p>
        Studies should therefore report which checked states they use: first
        check, final check, first correct check, all checks, or a
        feedback-specific segment. These choices answer different validity
        questions.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-feedback-policy-as-design-covariate">
      <title>Feedback Policy as a Design Covariate</title>

      <p>
        When comparing items, the feedback policy belongs among the item-design
        covariates, just as the modeling chapter includes it in the vector
        <m>\mathbf{z}_q</m>. Two items with the same numbers of cards and slots
        can produce systematically different trajectories if one guides revision
        and the other only grades it.
      </p>

      <p>
        When comparing semesters, the policy should be checked for source or
        platform changes. A shift in trajectory features across offerings may
        reflect a changed feedback environment rather than a changed student
        population. That is one instance of the source-version discipline below.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-item-design-effects">
    <title>Item Design Effects</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Item design determines which responses are possible and which errors are
        observable. Features cannot be interpreted apart from the design that
        produced them.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-response-space-effects">
      <title>Response Space Effects</title>

      <p>
        The <xref ref="gt-configuration-space" text="custom">configuration
        space</xref> grows as
        <m>|\mathcal{C}|=(R+1)^P</m>
        (<xref ref="an-configuration-space" text="custom">the state-space
        definition</xref>): the four-card running question has <m>256</m> states,
        while larger items have many more. Larger response spaces admit more
        distinct trajectories, more wrong states, and more ways to be partially
        correct. Raw move counts, check counts, and
        <xref ref="gt-distance" text="custom">distances</xref> are therefore not
        directly comparable across items of different sizes.
      </p>

      <p>
        Baseline-corrected progress, item-design covariates, and within-item
        comparisons reduce the problem without removing it. The design always
        shapes the behavior.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-decoy-design-effects">
      <title>Decoys and Observable Errors</title>

      <p>
        Decoys create observability. An item without a decoy card cannot show a
        decoy-card leak; an item without a decoy slot cannot show a decoy-slot
        leak. The absence of a feature may therefore reflect the absence of an
        opportunity, not the absence of a student tendency. Rates of decoy errors
        are comparable only across items that offered comparable opportunities.
      </p>

      <p>
        Conversely, a high decoy-use rate may indicate a decoy doing its intended
        diagnostic work, or it may reflect visual layout, wording, category
        salience, or an assumption about the interface. Like the diagnostic flags
        of the visualization chapter, such rates are prompts for review, not
        automatic conclusions (<xref ref="subsec-diagnostic-flags" text="title"/>):
        the display identifies the pattern; interpretation requires returning to
        the item.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-layout-and-affordance-effects">
      <title>Layout and Interface Affordances</title>

      <p>
        The mathematical model treats zones abstractly, but the student sees a
        rendered board. Spatial layout, card order, slot order, screen size,
        scrolling, drag mechanics, and visual prominence all influence behavior.
        A card may land in a nearby wrong slot for reasons of geometry rather
        than content; a decoy slot may attract responses by prominence; a long
        action gap may reflect difficulty dragging rather than difficulty
        reasoning.
      </p>

      <p>
        Where possible, item diagnostics should be read alongside the
        <xref ref="subsec-rendered-board" text="custom">rendered item</xref>, not
        only the source and the logs.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-source-version-effects">
      <title>Source Version Effects</title>

      <p>
        Small source changes have large analytic consequences. Reordering cards
        silently renumbers the <xref ref="gt-positional-labels" text="custom">
        positional labels</xref> that the <xref ref="gt-raw-data" text="custom">raw
        data</xref> use (<xref ref="subsec-source-order" text="title"/>).
        Rewording a prompt changes the task. Changing feedback changes the
        strategic environment. Adding or removing a decoy changes the response
        space itself.
      </p>

      <p>
        The processing chapter's item-map versioning
        (<xref ref="subsec-source-versioning" text="title"/>) exists for this
        reason: comparisons across semesters must distinguish stable items from
        revised ones, and an item version is part of every feature's identity.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-validation-strategies">
    <title>Validation Strategies</title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Validation asks whether features and interpretations behave as expected
        under additional evidence. Different claims call for different strategies,
        and the six below are complementary rather than interchangeable.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-internal-validation">
      <title>Internal Validation</title>

      <p>
        Internal validation checks that the analytic objects are constructed
        coherently: check anchors agree with replayed states
        (<xref ref="an-check-anchor-consistency" text="custom">the trajectory
        model's anchor condition</xref>), every reconstructed state is a valid
        membership matrix, first-check, final-check, and all-check summaries are
        mutually consistent, heatmaps agree with per-card correctness summaries,
        and conclusions are examined for sensitivity to idle-time thresholds and
        attempt-boundary rules.
      </p>

      <p>
        Internal validation establishes no cognitive interpretation. It
        establishes that the framework's own machinery, parsing, replay,
        aggregation, and filtering, was applied consistently. Every later
        argument presupposes that consistency.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-convergent-validation">
      <title>Convergent Validation</title>

      <p>
        Convergent validation asks whether drag-and-drop features align with
        other measures they should relate to: scores on related questions,
        written explanations of the same classification task, instructor-coded
        categories, later transfer items, or course-level measures of
        preparation.
      </p>

      <p>
        For example, if a decoy-slot pattern is interpreted as confusion between
        two mathematical categories, then written work should show the same
        confusion more often among students who exhibit the pattern. If it does
        not, the interpretation, not the feature, is what fails.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-discriminant-validation">
      <title>Discriminant Validation</title>

      <p>
        Discriminant validation asks whether features avoid measuring what they
        should not. A grouping-structure feature should not be driven by screen
        size, page position, or logging artifacts. A feedback-use feature should
        not be computed as though incompatible feedback policies exposed the same
        information; this is the undefined-versus-zero distinction of
        <xref ref="subsec-feedback-sensitive-features-validity" text="title"/>.
      </p>

      <p>
        This strategy matters most for process features, because process traces
        are especially sensitive to interface and implementation details.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-known-groups-validation">
      <title>Known-Groups and Designed-Contrast Validation</title>

      <p>
        If groups or conditions are expected to differ, the framework should
        detect the difference. An item designed with a strong decoy should show
        more decoy interaction than a parallel item without it. A card-level
        feedback version of an item should show different post-check revision
        patterns than an all-or-nothing version.
      </p>

      <p>
        Designed contrasts are especially valuable because they connect features
        to deliberate design choices. They test whether a feature is sensitive to
        the construct it is being used to represent.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-cross-context-validation">
      <title>Cross-Context Validation</title>

      <p>
        Patterns local to one cohort may not generalize. Cross-context validation
        checks stability across semesters, sections, instructors, institutions,
        item versions, and feedback policies, using the validation splits
        formalized in the modeling chapter
        (<xref ref="subsec-validation-splits" text="title"/>).
      </p>

      <p>
        Stable patterns are stronger candidates for item-level or construct-level
        interpretation. Patterns that vary across contexts may still matter, but
        the context becomes part of the interpretation.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="subsec-sensitivity-analysis">
      <title>Sensitivity Analysis</title>

      <p>
        Many features depend on processing and comparison choices made earlier in
        the book: the idle threshold <m>\delta_{\max}</m>, first-check versus
        final-check states, inclusion of traces with reconstruction warnings, the
        attempt-boundary rule, the tray's treatment in grouping metrics
        (<xref ref="subsec-tray-in-merge-split" text="title"/>), and labeled
        versus label-free comparison. Sensitivity analysis reruns the conclusion
        across reasonable settings of these choices.
      </p>

      <p>
        A conclusion that survives reasonable analytic variation is robust. A
        conclusion that depends on one fragile processing decision is a finding
        about the decision.
      </p>
    </subsection>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-framework-retrospective">
    <title>Looking Back Across the Framework</title>

    <p>
      This book began with a raw log and a small question about shapes, then built
      upward one deliberate layer at a time. The anatomy chapter fixed what the
      platform records (<xref ref="ch-dnd-anatomy"/>). The item-design layer
      turned the authored question into index sets, a key, decoys, and a feedback
      policy (<xref ref="ch-item-design"/>). The state model gave one board
      snapshot a configuration and a membership matrix
      (<xref ref="ch-state-model"/>), and the trajectory model set those states
      in motion as marked, timestamped walks through the configuration graph
      (<xref ref="ch-event-trajectory-model"/>). The feature layer gave states
      and trajectories a stable descriptive vocabulary
      (<xref ref="ch-baseline-features"/>); the processing layer made that
      vocabulary computable from real exports, with quality flags in place of
      silent repairs (<xref ref="ch-data-processing"/>). Diagnostics made items
      visible (<xref ref="ch-visualization-diagnostics"/>), advanced comparisons
      separated grouping from naming and let errors carry structure
      (<xref ref="ch-group-features"/>), and the modeling layer
      asked which patterns are stable, predictive, and explainable
      (<xref ref="ch-statistical-modeling"/>).
    </p>

    <p>
      Each layer added power on one condition: honesty about what the layer does
      not know. The state model does not know how the board got that way; the
      trajectory does not know why the student moved; the features are movement
      descriptions, not direct evidence; the diagnostics are prompts for review,
      not automatic conclusions; and the models are associations until a design
      supports more. This chapter's claim ladder assembles those conditions into
      a working discipline: observation, structural feature, response pattern,
      interpretation, validated claim. The language of a report should not outrun
      the level actually reached.
    </p>

    <p>
      Used this way, the framework is a research instrument rather than a scoring
      convenience. The strongest workflow it supports is layered like the book:
      fix the design and state model; reconstruct trajectories and document their
      quality; compute descriptive and advanced features; visualize and model the
      patterns; and interpret only as far as the validation evidence reaches.
    </p>

    <p>
      For reference while doing that work, the backmatter collects the notation in
      one place (<xref ref="appendix-notation"/>) and the technical and
      group-facing vocabulary in the glossary
      (<xref ref="sec-glossary-terms" text="title"/>). The running four-shape
      question was kept deliberately small so every object in the framework could
      be verified by hand. Applying the same machinery at scale, across items,
      cohorts, and semesters, is the research program this framework was built to
      support.
    </p>
  </section>
</chapter>
